// The Control Gallery's index and prose.
//
// One record per specimen. The (key) MUST match a `# >>gallery: <key>` marker in
// gallery.mast, gallery_pages.mast or gallery_specimens.py -- that key is the
// join between the prose here and the code there, and a record naming a marker
// that does not exist shows up as "no marked span" in the source panel.
//
// FILE ORDER IS NAV ORDER, and consecutive records sharing a Category collapse
// under one header in the index. So the shape of this file is the shape of the
// menu.
//
// First line of the body is the BLURB (the header line). Everything after it is
// NOTES, rendered by gui_text_area -- so `-` bullets and `#` headings work.
//
// Kind: trap  -> runs BROKEN next to FIXED, and needs <key>_broken/<key>_fixed spans
// Kind: page  -> drawn full-bleed on the Gallery Viewer console

# [Gallery](gallery)

## [gui_text](text_basic)
---
Category: Controls
---
One styled line. $text: comes first; justify:left is the default.

A single styled line of text. For anything multi-line or formatted, reach for
gui_text_area instead -- it parses a mini-markdown and scrolls itself.
- `$text:` must come FIRST in the style string.
- `justify:left` is the default. Never write it.
- Give text rows about 2.2em or more; cramped vertical text is the most common
  first review note on a new panel.

## [gui_text (live)](text_live)
---
Category: Controls
---
Keep the handle and update it. .update() replaces the WHOLE style string.

A plain gui_text is built once and then goes stale. For a value that moves, keep
the widget it returns and update that.
- `.update()` REPLACES the entire style string. Pass only `text:` and the font,
  color and justification go with it.
- This is the light alternative to a full repaint. See the watch/repaint recipe
  for when the state changes in another task entirely.

## [gui_button](button_basic)
---
Category: Controls
---
on gui_message fires when the value changes.

- Use `on_press=` / `data=` rather than an `on gui_message` block when the button
  is built inside a `for` loop. See the "handler in a for loop" trap.
- `data={}` entries are injected as task VARIABLES in the handler, and the widget
  that fired is `__ITEM__`.

## [gui_checkbox](checkbox_basic)
---
Category: Controls
---
State lives in your variable; the widget reflects it.

The checkbox does not own its state -- your variable does, and the widget is
built from it. `var=` binds the two so a toggle writes back for you.

## [gui_drop_down](drop_down_basic)
---
Category: Controls
---
list: is comma separated. var= binds the choice to a variable.

Prefer a dropdown over a row of buttons once there are more than about four
choices; it costs one row instead of four.

## [gui_int_slider](slider_basic)
---
Category: Controls
---
low/high in the props; read .value in the handler.

A slider declines to be measured (its size includes engine-drawn chrome), so a
`col-width: content` column holding one falls back to flex rather than to zero.

## [gui_list_box](list_box_basic)
---
Category: Controls
---
Every repeating list. item_template renders a row, title_template labels it.

The settled pattern for anything that repeats: a listbox plus a detail panel
acting on the selection.
- Label it with `title_template`, NOT a text row above it, so the list gets the
  whole section.
- In the item template, size the ROWS and return None. Returning a size leaves
  the item section degenerate, which kills selection and the click region.
- See the item_template shelf under Recipes for four row layouts to start from.

## [gui_table](table_basic)
---
Category: Controls
---
A listbox with columns. The declarative form generates the row for you.

Two forms: pass column specs and it builds the row, or use it as a context
manager and author the row yourself.
- `auto` columns are sized to the widest cell, header included, and share
  whatever percent the fixed columns leave.
- Cells can be interactive (checkbox, dropdown, input, button); the row is
  updated before `on_cell_change` is called.

## [gui_property_list_box](property_list_box_basic)
---
Category: Controls
---
Name/value rows from a dict, each value an expression evaluated later.

The values are expression STRINGS, evaluated by the list box rather than where
you wrote them -- so they cannot see the locals of the task that built the
panel. Referencing one is a "name is not defined" at present time. Keep them
self-contained.

## [gui_text_area](text_area_basic)
---
Category: Controls
---
Multi-line rich text with mini-markdown, and it scrolls itself.

Not simply a bigger gui_text. It parses a markdown-like language and adds a
vertical scrollbar when the content overflows.
- `#`/`##`/`###` headings, `-` bullets, `1.` ordered lists, blank line resets.
- `^` is the newline.
- Inline objects: `![](image://key)`, `[](ship://hull)`, `[](face://...)`.
- It CANNOT render source code: `#` is also a MAST comment, `-` a bullet, and
  `$` a style directive. Render code as one gui_text per line.

## [gui_icon](icon_basic)
---
Category: Controls
---
By sheet index, or by NAME so a mission can re-skin it.

Prefer the name form. A name follows its alias to a look, and a mission that
registers its own art re-skins every screen that draws that name.
- An unknown name draws NOTHING and says so once. A wrong icon is worse than a
  missing one, because it looks deliberate.

## [gui_icon_button](icon_button_basic)
---
Category: Controls
---
A clickable icon. Fires gui_click, not gui_message.

`on gui_click` is for icons and anything carrying a `click_tag`; `on gui_message`
is for widgets whose VALUE changes.

## [gui_radio / gui_vradio](radio_basic)
---
Category: Controls
---
One of N, horizontal or vertical. var= binds the choice.

Labels are one comma-separated string. Use it when all the choices are worth
showing at once; otherwise a dropdown costs less room.

## [gui_input](input_basic)
---
Category: Controls
---
Typed text. var= pre-fills it and takes the value back.

The browser mock reports a typein twice: `change` per keystroke with the
cumulative string, and `submit` on Enter.

## [gui_slider](slider_float)
---
Category: Controls
---
The float slider. min/max/label live in the props.

`gui_int_slider` is the same widget restricted to whole numbers -- which is what
you usually want for a count.

## [gui_grid](grid_basic)
---
Category: Controls
---
A context manager that wraps every N items to a new row.

Items flow left to right and wrap automatically, so a fixed-column layout needs
no manual `gui_row()` per line. The short final row is padded so the columns stay
aligned. It only starts standard rows -- no new rendering path.

## [gui_face](face_basic)
---
Category: Controls
---
A generated crew portrait from a face string.

The `faces` module builds them per race (`random_terran`, `random_skaraan`, ...).
Store the string if you want the same face twice; generating one per repaint
gives you a different person every frame.

## [gui_ship](ship_basic)
---
Category: Controls
---
A hull rendered from its ship_data key.

Like the slider, a Ship declines to be measured -- its size includes engine-drawn
chrome -- so it falls back to flex in a content-sized column rather than to zero.

## [gui_image](image_basic)
---
Category: Controls
---
Prefer an ATLAS KEY. Naming the art once is what lets a mission re-skin it.

`gui_image_add_atlas(key, file)` registers a name, and any command taking image
properties then accepts that key. A mission re-skins every screen that draws it
by re-registering the key -- the same argument as icons by name. Passing the
file directly works and is fine for a one-off, but nothing else can re-skin it.

- Art is addressed WITHOUT its extension; the engine appends it.
- `media_shared("name")` resolves against this mission's `media/` first, then
  every pack declared under `shared_media:` in story.json. This gallery draws
  LegendaryMissions art it pins and never copies.
- An atlas key can name a CELL of a sheet, and the rect is in **pixels**
  (left, top, right, bottom), not percent.
- `fit`: 0 stretch, 1 absolute pixels, 2 keep aspect (top-left), 3 keep aspect
  (centered). Stretching art into a box it does not match is the usual reason a
  panel looks wrong.

## [gui_blank / gui_hole](blank_hole)
---
Category: Controls
---
Spacers. blank fills a cell; hole reserves columns so the next item spans them.

`gui_blank()` is one empty cell. `gui_hole(n)` reserves n column slots, so the
item after it spans them -- the way to make one cell wider than its neighbors
without computing percentages.

## [row-height modes](layout_row_modes)
---
Category: Layout
---
1fr shares the leftover, content hugs the text, a fixed em/px is taken off the top.

`1fr` is the DEFAULT: a row that declares nothing still shares the leftover space
but is never squeezed below its min-content.
- `content` = the tallest cell at its final width, wrapping included.
- A content row CANNOT invent space. Fill a section with fixed rows and the
  content rows are correctly squeezed to nothing -- that is the layout, not a bug.
- `em` is one line of the ROW's font, and an unfonted row is gui-2 (24px).

## [col-width modes](layout_col_modes)
---
Category: Layout
---
content is natural width, min-content the widest unbreakable word.

- `content` = natural width, clamped to what is available.
- `min-content` = the widest unbreakable word.
- `max-content` = the whole line, unwrapped.
- A plain number is a PERCENT of the enclosing section, not of the screen.

When a row cannot hold everything, flex columns shrink to zero first, then
content columns shrink toward min-content, then it clamps.

## [size arithmetic](layout_arithmetic)
---
Category: Layout
---
1em+10px and 62-25px both work.

Before v1.4.0 a `+`/`-` term was silently dropped, which is worth knowing if you
are reading older code that looks like it should work.

Padding is `left, top, right, bottom`, and top/bottom come OUT of the row height.
A single value (`padding:13px`) is horizontal only and costs no height.

## [overflow](layout_overflow)
---
Category: Layout
---
spill (default), shrink, ellipsis, hide -- for text that cannot fit at any size.

Applied at present time against the final rect. The engine does NOT clip, so
`spill` draws straight over whatever sits next to it.

This is not a substitute for sizing the row properly -- reach for it only when
the text genuinely cannot fit at any size.

## [watch / repaint](recipe_watch_repaint)
---
Category: Recipes
---
A panel cannot see state change in another task, so watch it and repaint.

`on change <expr>` takes any expression, not just a variable -- a function call,
a data_set read, a computed value.

The heavier form is a `gui_sub_task_schedule` watcher that polls and calls
`gui_task_jump("repaint")`. Same shape, more machinery; use it when the check is
expensive or needs its own delay loop.

## [status line](recipe_status_line)
---
Category: Recipes
---
Feedback must land on a surface that is actually shown.

A console with no info panel silently drops `comms_info_card` and info-panel
messages, and the player sees nothing at all. A panel that confirms its own
actions should own a visible status line.

Route chatter and notifications to the info panel; keep the text waterfall for
mechanical status.

## [item_template shelf](recipe_item_templates)
---
Category: Recipes
---
Four row templates to start from. Swapping the template is the only difference.

The listbox, the data and the selection are untouched between these four -- only
`item_template` changes.
- plain: a name and a value.
- banded: a background per row, decided per item.
- icon+label+value: `col-width: content` on the first two so values line up.
- detail: a heading plus a wrapping `content` row under it.

Every one sizes the ROW and returns None.

## [reusable style](recipe_style_def)
---
Category: Recipes
---
gui_style_def once, then hand it to every row.

One place to change the look, and the rows stop repeating a style string that
will eventually disagree with itself.

## [update() drops the style](trap_update_style)
---
Category: Traps
Kind: trap
---
update() REPLACES the whole style string.

Pass only `text:` and the font, color and justification go with it -- the widget
reverts to unstyled, left-aligned. Carry the whole string every time.

## [handler in a for loop](trap_loop_handler)
---
Category: Traps
Kind: trap
---
An inline on-block in a loop captures the loop var at its LAST value.

`on gui_message(...)` registered inside a `for` loop is flaky, and an inline
block closes over the loop variable at its final value -- so every button
reports the last one.

Proven fixes: `on_press=`/`data=` on the button, or read `__ITEM__` in the block
instead of the loop variable. For comms `+` buttons in a loop, use a handler
label and a data dict, never an inline block.

## [1em under a bigger font](trap_row_em_font)
---
Category: Traps
Kind: trap
---
em is one line of the ROW's font, and an unfonted row is gui-2.

One line by font: smallest 18, gui-1 22, gui-2 24, gui-3 28, gui-4 32, gui-5 36,
gui-6 52. Declare the font on the row, or size the row to `content`.

## [padding eats row height](trap_padding_height)
---
Category: Traps
Kind: trap
---
Top and bottom padding come OUT of the row height.

A row needing one gui-3 line plus 10px of top padding is `1em+10px`, not `1em`.
A single-value padding is horizontal only and costs no height.

## [content row starved](trap_content_starved)
---
Category: Traps
Kind: trap
---
A content row cannot invent space.

Fill a section with fixed rows and the content rows are correctly squeezed to
nothing. If a content row renders zero-height, the section is oversubscribed --
that is the layout working, not a bug. Give the panel room.

## [engine widget + overlay strip](page_engine_widget)
---
Category: Full page
Kind: page
---
gui_layout_widget embeds a real engine view; your rows draw over it.

`2dview`, `comms_2d_view`, `science_2d_view`, `weapon_2d_view`, `ship_data`,
`red_alert`, `text_waterfall` and more. `gui_console("helm")` sets a whole
standard console's widget list at once.

Detached command consoles (a game master, an admiral overseer) are built this
way -- a camera object plus embedded 2D views.

## [gui_region, updated in place](page_region)
---
Category: Full page
Kind: page
---
An absolutely-positioned region redraws by itself.

The page underneath is not repainted, which is what makes a live HUD cheap.
- Rebuild the WHOLE region and fill it again. Updating one child in place inside
  an absolute region ghosts in the ENGINE (the mock cannot reproduce it).
- Leaving the `with` block re-presents the region for you. Do NOT call
  `represent()`: it takes an event, and `EVENT` is a ROUTE variable that does not
  exist inside a GUI task's on-change.

## [listbox + detail console](page_master_detail)
---
Category: Full page
Kind: page
---
The settled console shape: a titled listbox acting on a detail panel.

Map, research, fleets, requisition, the hangar board and the quest log are all
this shape. Title the list with `title_template`, and give the detail panel the
room its content needs rather than a strip.

## [layout playground](page_layout_playground)
---
Category: Full page
Kind: page
---
Set row-height, col-width and font from dropdowns and watch the boxes move.

The style strings the dropdowns assemble are echoed above the boxes, because
reading the string next to the result is most of the lesson.

A fixed row sits above the row under test and a `1fr` row below it, so a starved
content row reads as exactly that rather than as a mystery.
